From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 23:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] ppc64: fix PPC_xsmaxdp/PPC_xsmindp constants emitting ISA 3.0

The XX3-form opcode constants for the ISA 2.06 base-form scalar min/max
hold the encodings of the ISA 3.0 (POWER9) Type-C instructions instead:

    PPC_xsmaxdp = 0xF0000400   is xsmaxcdp   (XO=128)
    PPC_xsmindp = 0xF0000440   is xsmincdp   (XO=136)

The XX3 XO field is at bits 21-28, so the constant is XO << 3. The real
base forms are XO=160 and XO=168, i.e. 0xF0000500 and 0xF0000540. The
comment directly above the constants already says "Base (ISA 2.06)
forms", so this is a transcription error rather than a design choice.

The consequence is that the POWER8 fallback in MacroAssembler-ppc64-inl.h
-- which is correctly gated behind !HasPOWER9() and correctly reached --
emits a POWER9 instruction. Every Math.min()/Math.max() on doubles that
Ion compiles therefore SIGILLs on POWER8. Observed on a POWER8 machine
running firefox 154.0.1 as:

    Program terminated with signal SIGILL, Illegal instruction.
    => 0xfce5f58a96c:  xsmincdp vs1,vs1,vs2

which is exactly what as_xsmindp(srcDest, srcDest, other) assembles to
with srcDest=vs1, other=vs2 under the wrong constant. POWER9 is
unaffected because HasPOWER9() selects the xsminjdp/xsmaxjdp path.

Simulator-ppc64.cpp decodes the same two instructions and carries the
same error in its case labels (XO8 128/136 rather than 160/168), so the
simulator would mis-decode the corrected encodings; fixed alongside.

Upstreamable: yes -- this is a bug in runlevel5's firefox-ppc64 patchset
(0004-Add-PPC64LE-JIT-backend.patch), not a local workaround.
---
--- a/js/src/jit/ppc64/Assembler-ppc64.h
+++ b/js/src/jit/ppc64/Assembler-ppc64.h
@@ -777,8 +777,8 @@
   // Base (ISA 2.06) forms: NaN operands are suppressed in favor of the
   // numeric operand (minNum/maxNum style), so these are only ECMA-correct
   // once NaN inputs have been excluded.
-  PPC_xsmaxdp = 0xF0000400,
-  PPC_xsmindp = 0xF0000440,
+  PPC_xsmaxdp = 0xF0000500,
+  PPC_xsmindp = 0xF0000540,
   PPC_xxbrd = 0xF017076C,
   PPC_xxbrq = 0xF01F076C,  // VSX vector byte-reverse quadword (whole 16 bytes)
   PPC_xvabsdp = 0xF0000764,
--- a/js/src/jit/ppc64/Simulator-ppc64.cpp
+++ b/js/src/jit/ppc64/Simulator-ppc64.cpp
@@ -6055,8 +6055,8 @@
     // a QNaN operand loses to the numeric operand (minNum/maxNum style);
     // both-QNaN returns src1; ±0 ties follow signed-zero ordering
     // (max -> +0, min -> -0).
-    case 256: case 257:    // xsmaxdp  (XO8=128 → 9-bit 256/257)
-    case 272: case 273: {  // xsmindp  (XO8=136 → 9-bit 272/273)
+    case 320: case 321:    // xsmaxdp  (XO8=160 → 9-bit 320/321)
+    case 336: case 337: {  // xsmindp  (XO8=168 → 9-bit 336/337)
       int xa = int(instr->raValue() | (instr->bit(2) << 5));
       int xb = int(instr->rbValue() | (instr->bit(1) << 5));
       int xt = int(instr->rtValue() | (instr->bit(0) << 5));
@@ -6066,7 +6066,7 @@
       double a, b;
       memcpy(&a, ab + 8, 8);
       memcpy(&b, bb + 8, 8);
-      bool isMax = (xo >> 1) == 128;
+      bool isMax = (xo >> 1) == 160;
       auto isSNaN = [](double v) {
         uint64_t bits;
         memcpy(&bits, &v, 8);
